  "question": {
    "text": "The earth's magnetic field lines resemble that of a dipole at the centre of the earth. If the magnetic moment of this dipole is close to $8 \\times 10^{22} \\mathrm{Am}^2$, the value of earth's magnetic field near the equator is close to (radius of the earth $\\left.=6.4 \\times 10^6 \\mathrm{~m}\\right)$"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n$0.6$ Gauss\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n $1.2$ Gauss\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n $1.8$ Gauss\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n $0.32$ Gauss",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given $M=8 \\times 10^{22} \\mathrm{Am}^2$\n$$\n\\mathrm{d}=\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{e}}=6.4 \\times 10^6 \\mathrm{~m}\n$$\nEarth's magnetic field, $\\mathrm{B}=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4 \\pi} \\cdot \\frac{2 \\mathrm{M}}{\\mathrm{d}^3}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&=\\frac{4 \\pi \\times 10^{-7}}{4 \\pi} \\times \\frac{2 \\times 8 \\times 10^{22}}{\\left(6.4 \\times 10^6\\right)^3} \\\\\n&\\cong 0.6 \\text { Gauss }\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  }
}